WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 89: The DuPont Powder Mills: Black Powder, Chemistry, and the Brandywine

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:17.360
[Rebecca] In the autumn of 1800, a young French immigrant nobleman named Eluthur Eren DuPont went on a hunting trip in the forests of New Jersey with an American friend.

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:27.200
[Rebecca] When their guns repeatedly misfired and produced poor patterns, DuPont was shocked by the abysmal quality and exorbitant price of American black powder.

5
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.840
[Rebecca] DuPont was no ordinary hunter.

6
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:38.880
[Rebecca] In Paris, he had studied chemistry under Anton Levoisier, the father of modern chemistry and the master of the French royal gunpowder mills.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:49.360
[Rebecca] Recognizing an immense commercial opportunity in his adopted country, DuPont bought 70 acres along the rushing waters of Brandywine Creek in northern Delaware.

8
00:00:49.360 --> 00:01:01.520
[Rebecca] There, he founded an industrial dynasty that fueled the construction of American canals, supplied the Union Army in the Civil War, and transformed into the greatest chemical company in the world.

9
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:08.560
[Rebecca] George brings us the explosive story of the DuPont Powder Mills.

10
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:16.560
[George] The origins of the DuPont Industrial Empire were forged in the intellectual brilliance and bloody chaos of revolutionary France.

11
00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:29.680
[George] Born in Paris in 1771, Eleuther Urené DuPont was the son of Pierre Samuel DuPont de Nemers, a prominent philosopher, economist, and political advisor to King Louis XVI.

12
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:40.320
[George] At age 17, young Eren entered the elite French Royal Gunpowder Agency at the arsenal in Paris, where he was mentored by the brilliant chemist Antoine Lavoisier.

13
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:54.880
[George] Lavoisier had revolutionized gunpowder manufacturing through rigorous scientific methodology, refining potassium nitrate to 99% purity and transforming France's black powder into the finest military propellant in Europe.

14
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.800
[George] But the French Revolution soon turned radical and murderous.

15
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:10.920
[George] In 1794, during the reign of terror, Lavoisier was sent to the guillotine by Jacobin tribunals, while Pierre DuPont was imprisoned, barely escaping the guillotine when Robespierre fell.

16
00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:28.440
[George] Facing financial ruin, political persecution, and royalist street violence, the DuPont family made the bold decision to liquidate their remaining assets and cross the Atlantic to the United States in 1800, seeking freedom and honest enterprise in the young American Republic.

17
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:40.280
[George] Erené DuPont father Pierre was a celebrated French physiocrat and royal advisor whose intimate friendship with Thomas Jefferson opened doors for the aristocratic family when they arrived in the United States.

18
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:51.960
[George] Anton Lavoisier's rigorous scientific training at the Paris Arsenal instilled in young Eren an obsession with chemical purity and precision measurement that would become the hallmark of the DuPont Company.

19
00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:59.720
[George] Under the mentorship of Anton Lavoisier, young Eren learned the exact chemical proportions required for clean burning gunpowder.

20
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:07.400
[George] Refined sulfur and potassium nitrate were tested rigorously in on-site laboratories to guarantee absolute chemical purity.

21
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:15.640
[George] Shortly after settling in New Jersey, Erené DuPont went bird hunting with a wealthy American veteran named Colonel Louis de Towsard.

22
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:24.280
[George] When their shotguns fouled with thick sulfuric soot and repeatedly failed to discharge, DuPont inspected the powder horns and was appalled.

23
00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:37.640
[George] American gunpowder was crude, smoky, unstable, and dangerously inconsistent, manufactured in primitive backwoods mills that failed to purify their saltpeter or properly grind sulfur and charcoal.

24
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:49.760
[George] Furthermore, because American mills produced so little high-grade powder, the nation was dangerously dependent on expensive, imported British gunpowder, which cost more than double its true value.

25
00:03:50.080 --> 00:04:10.080
[George] DuPont realized that with his rigorous Lavoisier training, detailed architectural blueprints of French royal stamping mills, and advanced knowledge of chemical purification, he could manufacture the highest grade black powder in the Western Hemisphere, establishing American military independence and securing his family's fortune.

26
00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:22.160
[George] American black powder of the late 18th century was notorious for misfiring and fouling gun barrels with heavy soot, leaving hunters and milish men with unreliable, low-velocity ammunition.

27
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:34.640
[George] The realization that the young American Republic was almost completely dependent on imported British gunpowder convinced DuPont that domestic black powder manufacturing was essential to American national sovereignty.

28
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:44.160
[George] The inferior quality of domestic American gunpowder frustrated hunters, militia officers, and blasters, creating immense commercial opportunity.

29
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:51.840
[George] George Washington himself had previously recognized the strategic vulnerability of American reliance on foreign powder imports.

30
00:04:51.840 --> 00:05:09.840
[George] In the spring of 1801, DuPont surveyed potential manufacturing sites across the Mid Atlantic, searching for three critical geographic factors reliable water power, water transport to maritime shipping lanes, and an abundance of willow timber to produce high grade charcoal.

31
00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:17.200
[George] He found the ideal location four miles north of Wilmington, Delaware, along the rocky banks of Brandywine Creek.

32
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:31.880
[George] The Brandywine was an industrial powerhouse, descending over 100 feet in its final four miles to the Delaware River, the torrential current drove dozens of massive wooden water wheels that powered flour mills and textile factories.

33
00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:43.160
[George] Furthermore, the local Quaker farming community welcomed industrious French immigrants, and nearby French-speaking colonies in Philadelphia provided immediate access to skilled labor.

34
00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:57.000
[George] With financing from French investors and American friends, DuPont purchased 70 acres of rocky hillside farmland for $6,700 in 1802, christening the enterprise the Eleuthurian Mills.

35
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:09.160
[George] The rushing waters of Brandywine Creek descended over a hundred feet through a narrow rocky gorge, providing torrential water power that drove dozens of commercial flour, textile, and paper mills.

36
00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:20.920
[George] The local Quaker community along the Brandywine welcomed the French immigrant family, providing skilled stonemasons and millwrights who helped construct the massive granite race walls and water wheels.

37
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:29.560
[George] The swift rapids of the Brandywine River, flowing through deep granite gorges, provided relentless water power for massive roll mills.

38
00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:36.760
[George] Heavy granite millstones imported from France crushed the sulfur and charcoal mixture with measured, rhythmic precision.

39
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.840
[George] Manufacturing black powder was an inherently lethal enterprise.

40
00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:52.920
[George] A single stray spark from an iron horseshoe, a brass hammer, or friction on a drive belt could instantly ignite tons of explosives, obliterating buildings and workers in a fraction of a second.

41
00:06:52.920 --> 00:07:05.320
[George] To minimize loss of life and prevent chain reaction explosions, Erenet DuPont designed an ingenious, revolutionary architectural layout that became famous worldwide as the DuPont mill design.

42
00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:19.520
[George] Individual powder manufacturing buildings, incorporating mills, pressing rooms, and graining mills, were constructed as separate stone structures spaced hundreds of feet apart along the creek bank, separated by massive earthen blast berms.

43
00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:31.760
[George] Each mill was built with three walls of massive, three-foot-thick local brandywine blue granite, while the fourth wall facing the creek and the wooden roof were built of flimsy, lightweight boards.

44
00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:47.520
[George] When an accidental explosion occurred, the concussive force followed the path of least resistance, blowing the roof and river wall harmlessly out over the water, while the thick stone side walls protected neighboring workers and adjacent mill buildings from flying debris.

45
00:07:47.520 --> 00:08:00.560
[George] DuPont revolutionary three-sided granite mill design directed accidental blast waves out over the river waters, protecting adjacent buildings and establishing an unprecedented standard for industrial safety.

46
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:12.560
[George] The massive earthen blast berms separating each mill building were planted with willow trees, providing root stabilization against erosion while supplying premium wood for burning high-purity charcoal.

47
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:21.440
[George] The mill complexes were spaced along the riverbank, surrounded by earthen revetments designed to direct accidental blasts upward away from workers.

48
00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:28.640
[George] Every workman was required to wear soft wooden-pegged shoes to eliminate the lethal risk of iron sparks on stone floors.

49
00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:38.480
[George] By 1804, the Eleuthurian mills began rolling out their first commercial barrels of black powder, stamped with the famous DuPont Eagle trademark.

50
00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:46.400
[George] Customers marveled at the powder's superior burning rate, high muzzle velocity, and clean combustion, and sales exploded.

51
00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:55.040
[George] In 1811, as war with Great Britain loomed, President Thomas Jefferson personally recommended DuPont powder to the War Department.

52
00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:02.440
[George] During the War of 1812, the DuPont Mills became the primary gunpowder arsenal of the United States military.

53
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:12.680
[George] Supplying over 500,000 pounds of high-grade artillery and musket powder to Commodore Oliver Hazard Perry on Lake Erie and General Andrew Jackson at New Orleans.

54
00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:18.520
[George] Following the war, DuPont powder fueled the greatest infrastructure boom in American history.

55
00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:33.000
[George] Millions of pounds of DuPont blasting powder were detonated by crews cutting the Erie Canal through solid limestone, blasting tunnels for the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad, and clearing coal and iron mines across the Appalachian Mountains.

56
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:45.640
[George] During the War of 1812, DuPont Powder propelled Commodore Perry naval guns on Lake Erie and General Jackson Artillery at New Orleans, securing American military victory against Great Britain.

57
00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:58.440
[George] DuPont blasting powder was essential to the expansion of industrial America, clearing rock cuts for the Erie Canal and blasting railroad tunnels through the Appalachian mountain ranges throughout the 19th century.

58
00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:07.400
[George] During the War of 1812, DuPont supplied over half a million pounds of powder to the military, earning commendations for ballistic quality.

59
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:14.920
[George] Naval Commodores like Oliver Hazard Perry credited DuPont powder with providing the decisive range advantage on Lake Erie.

60
00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:22.040
[George] Yet despite the safety architecture, working in the powder yards required nerves of steel and constant vigilance.

61
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:35.240
[George] Workers were required to change into wool clothes with no metal buttons, wear soft wooden peg shoes or slippers with no iron nails, and hose down flagstone floors continuously to prevent dust accumulation.

62
00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:47.280
[George] Between 1802 and 1921, when black powder manufacturing ended on the brandywine, nearly 300 men, women, and children lost their lives in workplace explosions.

63
00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:59.520
[George] The worst disaster occurred in March 1818, when a catastrophic chain reaction explosion leveled the upper yards, killing 40 workers and leaving DuPont's wife Sophie permanently injured.

64
00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:03.040
[George] Yet the DuPont family never abandoned their workers.

65
00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:16.400
[George] Following every explosion, company policy dictated that the DuPont family pensionied widows, housed orphans, and paid medical expenses, while family members personally worked alongside laborers in the mills.

66
00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:23.680
[George] With Eren DuPont and his sons regularly risking their lives to repair damaged machinery and extinguish fires.

67
00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:27.280
[George] Working in the powder yards required extreme discipline.

68
00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:36.720
[George] Employees wore shoes with wooden pegs instead of iron nails and constantly hosed down stone floors to prevent friction sparks from igniting explosive dust.

69
00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:49.200
[George] The DuPont family policy of personally working alongside their laborers and pensioning widows of accident victims fostered an extraordinary bond of loyalty that spanned multiple generations of powder workers.

70
00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:57.840
[George] DuPont family members lived directly on the mill grounds, sharing constant dangers and establishing unprecedented standards of worker care.

71
00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:06.240
[George] Widows of fallen powdermen received lifelong pensions and company housing, fostering an extraordinary multi-generational family culture.

72
00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:21.120
[George] Throughout the 19th century, under the leadership of Eren's son Henry DuPont, the company expanded exponentially, supplying over 4 million pounds of powder to Union forces during the Civil War and pioneering smokeless powder and dynamite.

73
00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:31.400
[George] In the 20th century, the enterprise transitioned from black powder to advanced industrial chemistry, developing synthetic materials that redefined modern life.

74
00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:35.080
[George] Nylon, Teflon, Kevlar, and Neoprene.

75
00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:49.000
[George] In 1921, the original black powder mills on the Brandywine closed forever, and the property was preserved as the Hagley Museum and Library, an idyllic, 230-acre National Historic Landmark.

76
00:12:49.000 --> 00:13:02.440
[George] Today, visitors walk along the quiet, moss-covered stone walls of the original mill races, watching restored 19th-century water wheels turn massive iron roll stones in authentic demonstration mills.

77
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:17.800
[George] The rushing waters of Brandywine Creek continue to flow through the rocky Delaware Valley, an enduring monument to the French immigrant who combined revolutionary chemistry with frontier grit to build an industrial empire that fueled the growth of the American Republic.

78
00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:30.200
[George] The original black powder mills along the Brandywine are preserved today at the Hagley Museum, where restored water wheels and iron roll stones demonstrate the origins of modern American industrial chemistry.

79
00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:43.880
[George] From a single water-powered black powder mill on Brandywine Creek, DuPont evolved into a global scientific powerhouse, developing revolutionary materials that transformed 20th century science and daily life.

80
00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:52.440
[George] The original Hagley Mills on the Brandywine remain preserved today, celebrating the ingenuity and craftsmanship that built modern America.

81
00:13:52.440 --> 00:14:00.200
[George] Generations of chemical innovation that followed transformed the Brandywine Valley into the cradle of American industrial chemistry.

82
00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:07.800
[Rebecca] The DuPont Powder Mills harness the power of chemistry and water to shape the physical landscape of America.

83
00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:09.240
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

84
00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:16.720
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we investigate the revolutionary treason of Benedict Arnold and the capture of Major John Andre.

